"The Waking Stone, the core, is the story's protagonist and point of view."
"The core is a buried, half-sentient heartstone, a settlement core."
"The core is heavy, cold and down: a weight beneath the weight."
"The core perceives the world only through touch."
"The core feels weight, vibration and pressure through the bound soil that is its body."
"The core is blind and has no ears."
"The core knows day by the sun's warmth soaking down, and night by the cold rising from below."
"The core began the moment a settler's foot first pressed down onto the ground above it."
"The core woke because it was touched."
"The core did not wake into a known self, but surfaced out of a bottomless dark."
"Pulling at the thread of its past-life memory frightens the core."
"In its first hour the core learned not to pull the thread too hard."
"The core is not a point but a place: a held breadth of ground."
"The core cannot move, lift, buck, or send any signal up through the soil."
"The core's whole estate is to feel everything and do nothing."
"The core's deepest want is to matter to the small warm hand."
"The core wants not to be strong or feared, but to be more than dumb ground for that warmth."
"The core's second want is to remember its name."
"The core has a dim, impossible will."
"Who the core was before it became the stone, and how it died, is its central open mystery."
"Behind the core's waking trails a fraying thread, thin as the heat that lifts off stone at dusk."
"Pulling the thread yields only fragments."
"One fragment is a cold of a different kind, a wet and biting cold with edges to it."
"One fragment is a falling, the lurch of a body that has lost the ground beneath it."
"One fragment is a hand the core reached for and did not close around."
"One fragment is the grief of fingers closing on nothing."
"The fragments strongly suggest a death by falling, in cold and wet, reaching for someone's hand."
"No names, no place and no certainty are known of the core's past life."
"The core forgot its own name in the dark."
"The mystery of the lost name is wholly unresolved."
